{"parentUuid":"f887ce3b-d522-4ab2-b347-af146b6316e6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgGbZWGt4GCCwJp3Gy1CY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Committed 13e7a518 on `hedgerow-grass` (not pushed): distant enemy muzzle flashes now stay visible as a flickering star. That closes the item I left open last round. I checked it headless only, on still frames, a live firefight and `mv_mg.mp4` (sent above, a machine gun firing bursts from 40 m at exactly 60 fps), never in a real browser window.\n\n- **The problem:** a distant machine gun showed mostly as a ball of rain lit for three frames around a flash about 10 pixels across. That's easy to lose and reads more like a lamp than a gun.\n- **The flash:** a muzzle's star and flame tongues now never get smaller on screen than 14 and 9 pixels. Past that they grow and dim slightly, so a gun at 80 m still flickers as a flash. Bullet strikes on stone and steel keep at least 3 pixels. Measured on single flashes, the star now holds its size at 40 and 80 m.\n- **The rain:** a flash used to light a ball of rain like a lamp, at full strength, on every shot. A real flash lasts about a millisecond, so flash-lit rain is now a faint, small patch close around the muzzle, and the flash itself carries the shot.\n- **Live check:** in a real firefight at the bridge, the machine gun at the far end now shows as a distinct star, even with a flare up.\n- **Checks:** all three quality presets compile with no errors, the nine node tests pass, and the branch still merges cleanly with hedgerow-poc.\n\nThe test server and headless Chrome are stopped, and the shared notes have this turn's details."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":215,"cache_read_input_tokens":526975,"output_tokens":535,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web