{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"67899089a001388b4ee23fa635973a1e63b3d0151ea4b161a9bc86899177173c","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RrJxLqsgmLdQ6YhaYCmz8e","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"rendering rain streaks at night games lit by lights only visible backlit technique","mode":"standard"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013K9TEf3udkHFt1C4kSNwQP","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Call of Duty WWII night rain mission screenshot","mode":"standard"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"eea2d2efa9f3803020dc4f3ab94886968ada2c007f4aca8adc49bb7c546b4bf7","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013K9TEf3udkHFt1C4kSNwQP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Call of Duty WWII night rain mission screenshot\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Call of Duty: WWII Screenshots and Trailers\",\"url\":\"https://opencritic.com/game/4505/-/media\"},{\"title\":\"www.dsogaming.com\",\"url\":\"https://www.dsogaming.com/?p=98066\"},{\"title\":\"call of duty ww2 campaign details revealed\",\"url\":\"https://christianpost.com/news/call-of-duty-ww2-campaign-details-revealed.html\"},{\"title\":\"Call of Duty®: WWII\",\"url\":\"https://face.meei.harvard.edu/call-of-zombies-mobile-game/call-of-duty-r-wwii\"},{\"title\":\"LaunchBox Logo Back to Home Page\",\"url\":\"https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/details/79109\"},{\"title\":\"call of duty 2 screenshots\",\"url\":\"https://www.neowin.net/news/call-of-duty-2-screenshots/\"},{\"title\":\"gamesdb.launchbox-app.com\",\"url\":\"https://gamesdb.launchbox-app.com/games/details/6864\"},{\"title\":\"www.gameswelt.de\",\"url\":\"https://www.gameswelt.de/call-of-duty-wwii/bilder\"},{\"title\":\"call of duty wwii\",\"url\":\"https://www.co-op.gg/pc/game/call-of-duty-wwii\"}]\n\nThe search didn't turn up a screenshot of a night rain mission from Call of Duty: WWII. The results are mostly general media pages and campaign overviews.\n\n- **Media pages:** OpenCritic has a screenshots and trailers gallery for the game, but the entries are generic placeholders with no captions that identify a night or rain scene. (Call of Duty: WWII Screenshots and Trailers)\n- **Gameswelt:** It hosts a large image gallery with many screenshots, but the results don't describe individual images, so I can't confirm a night or rain scene there. (Call of Duty: WWII - Screenshots)\n- **Campaign context:** The game covers a squad from 1944 to 1945 in the European theater, with fighting in France, Belgium, and across the Rhine. That is the likeliest setting for a rainy night mission, but none of the results name one.\n\nTo find the specific image, I'd try searching for the mission name and \"screenshot\" or checking a dedicated gallery such as Gameswelt's or a wiki that captions its images.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RrJxLqsgmLdQ6YhaYCmz8e","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"rendering rain streaks at night games lit by lights only visible backlit technique\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Rethinking Nighttime Image Deraining via\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.17440v1\"},{\"title\":\"www.arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2510.17440\"},{\"title\":\"04b7126c370f175204442098356dc239 Paper Conference\",\"url\":\"https://papers.nips.REDACTED.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Paper 45 Efficient Rain Simulation based on Constrained View\",\"url\":\"https://ftp.saiconference.REDACTED.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Real-time Realistic Rain Rendering\",\"url\":\"https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/a64dff76-4d44-4827-8a3d-5c0e1b980deb/full\"},{\"title\":\"Real-time Realistic Rain Rendering\",\"url\":\"https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/a64dff76-4d44-4827-8a3d-5c0e1b980deb\"},{\"title\":\"www.cjig.cn\",\"url\":\"https://www.cjig.cn/en/article/doi/10.11834/jig.20130515/\"},{\"title\":\"api.crossref.org\",\"url\":\"https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1145%2F1141911.1141985\"},{\"title\":\"recercat.cat\",\"url\":\"https://recercat.cat/handle/2072/209683\"}]\n\nThe search turned up research on rain visibility under night lighting, and a few rendering papers that address the lighting dependence you're asking about.\n\n- **Rain shows up mainly near light sources.** A 2025 nighttime deraining paper notes that rain effects tend to cluster around lights and appear only under particular lighting. Synthetic datasets that add rain uniformly miss this, which hurts realism. ([arxiv 2510.17440](https://arxiv.org/html/2510.17440v1))\n- **Illumination-aware compositing.** That same work ties each rain mask to an illumination coefficient map of the night background, so rain brightens where the scene is lit. ([arxiv 2510.17440 PDF](https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2510.17440))\n- **Precomputed streak database.** One image-based method renders thousands of streaks across lighting and viewing directions, then adds them to a single image or video. It needs only a coarse depth map and light source properties. ([DOI 10.1145/1141911.1141985](https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1145%2F1141911.1141985))\n- **Monte Carlo ray tracing.** A Chinese journal paper traces rays through a raindrop mesh that oscillates over time, integrated into PBRT. It claims to handle any light source type. ([cjig.cn](https://www.cjig.cn/en/article/doi/10.11834/jig.20130515/))\n- **Real-time approach.** A UPC paper renders streaks with precomputed textures chosen by illumination parameters, and it supports artist-controlled density. ([UPC Commons](https://upcommons.upc.edu/entities/publication/a64dff76-4d44-4827-8a3d-5c0e1b980deb/full))\n\nFor a backlit night look, the practical takeaway is to make streaks appear only where they catch light from a source behind them. Scatter-lit or unlit regions should show little or no rain. I didn't find a source that describes this technique step by step, so that part is my inference.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]}}
