{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"009e2d238acaf8d3ded358f8220d673ac682d742c79e7040d373c7edebd949eb","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xk8oVVLS4qBDuBCF1AzvAk","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.17440v1","prompt":"What does the paper say about how rain looks in real nighttime images: where rain streaks are visible relative to light sources, how their brightness depends on the illumination/background, and why uniformly added synthetic rain looks unrealistic? Quote the key sentences (short) and list the figure numbers/URLs of figures showing real nighttime rain images."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"19705aadb8bd12ba68a01c589cb51756b3ff86d139ebde9de243d337ac5dda8f","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xk8oVVLS4qBDuBCF1AzvAk","type":"tool_result","content":"**How rain appears at night**\n\n- Rain is tied to light sources. The paper says \"rain in nighttime scenes is only visible near light sources and is not noticeable in low-illumination areas.\" (Sec. 3.1)\n- Rain is also hidden in the brightest areas: \"Rain is not only invisible in low-illumination areas but also in excessively high-illumination areas\" (Sec. 3.2).\n- Rain brightness depends on the background. Rain \"often reflects light from artificial sources, creating high-contrast patterns that stand out sharply against the dark background\" (Sec. 1). Paraphrasing Appendix B, rain streaks noticeably shift local illumination, which is why the Y channel shows the largest rain-related histogram change at night.\n\n**Why uniform synthetic rain looks unrealistic**\n\n- Earlier datasets add rain uniformly: \"random rain masks are added linearly to nighttime backgrounds\" (Sec. 1). The authors say this \"naturally leads to inadequate realism and harmony in the visual appearance\" (Sec. 1).\n- Paraphrasing Sec. 1, the authors argue uniform rain ignores the fact that real rain visibility varies with lighting, so it creates a domain gap with real images.\n\n**Figures showing real nighttime rain images**\n\n- Figure 6: real-world nighttime rainy images from RealRain-1k. Input paths: `2510.17440v1/figs/Fig6/1/01Input.jpg` and `2510.17440v1/figs/Fig6/2/01Input.jpg`\n- Figure 8: generalization results on real-world nighttime rainy images. Input path: `2510.17440v1/figs/Fig7/Input.jpg`\n- Figure 9: visual comparisons on real-world nighttime rainy images. Input path: `2510.17440v1/figs/Fig8/Input.jpg`\n- Figure 13(d): example \"Real\" images from HQ-NightRain (Appendix F). Path: `2510.17440v1/figs/Dataset_show/Real.jpg`\n\nThese paths are relative to the arXiv HTML page for 2510.17440v1. The page does not describe these real images in detail beyond these captions.\n\n[WebFetch note: this page's text is 106802 characters long and the answer above covers only characters 0 to 100000; the final 6802 were not read — to read on, call WebFetch again with the same url and offset: 100000.]"}]}}
