{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"c33bf617-35bd-4ff6-a907-5e7d748d7fbc","hash":"4dd84a6c4e4ede65460279e2f20f4310410ede01595ea5303995883ee2899232","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CKxAjCa1Q7vxRQFCThNP7f","name":"Edit","input":{"file_path":"/Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/.claude/worktrees/hedgerow-grass/fun/hedgerow/README.md","old_string":"    the start of the mission; when none burns, a lightning stroke drives the\n    same light from the bolt's direction),","new_string":"    the start of the mission; when none burns, a lightning stroke drives the\n    same light from the bolt's direction), `canopy.js` (the trees' shade\n    from that light where its shadow map does not reach: at load the\n    planted trees' crowns are laid on a 1 m map from above, how much sky\n    each metre of ground has over it in leaves - a pine or a fir thick, an\n    oak or a beech a little less, a birch thin, a dead tree hardly - and\n    the ground dims the flare's light by it, looked up where the light's\n    ray passes through the crowns ten metres up (in the vertex shader: the\n    ground's fragment shader has no texture unit to spare), from where the\n    shadow map ends and, on low quality, which draws no shadows for the\n    flare, everywhere, so the forest floor under a flare no longer glazes\n    pale as if it lay in the open),"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FcNWerg2dmbg7AoFGTVvYc","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/vojta/Dev/protocodus.github.io/.claude/worktrees/hedgerow-grass && git add fun/hedgerow/js/canopy.js fun/hedgerow/js/main.js fun/hedgerow/js/terrain.js fun/hedgerow/README.md && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\nHedgerow graphics: the forest canopy shades the floor under a flare\n\nThe flare's light reaches the ground through an 80 m shadow map\nfollowing the player. Beyond it the forest floor under the crowns was\nlit as if it lay in the open. Low quality draws no flare shadows at all,\nso there the whole forest floor took a pale silvery glaze under every\nflare: the snow-like look seen in earlier rounds.\n\ncanopy.js builds a top-down cover map at load: the planted trees'\ncrowns (vegetation.js placements, each kind's model box for its spread)\non a 1 m grid over the terrain. Cover per crown: pine/fir 0.85,\noak/beech 0.8, birch 0.55, dead tree 0.25, thinner towards the edge,\noverlaps compounding, softened over ~4 m. The terrain dims only the\nflare's directional light by it (picked out by its direction, as\nfill.js picks out the fill lights), looked up where the light's ray\npasses through the crowns 10 m up. Lightning drives the same light, so\nit gets the canopy too. The term takes over from the real shadow map\nover its last ~8 m and applies everywhere when the flare has no shadow\nmap. The lookup is in the vertex shader: the terrain's fragment shader\nalready uses all 16 texture units, and its vertices (0.5-1 m apart) are\nfiner than the map.\n\nUnder frozen bright flares, A/B'd in place: on low the lower half of a\nforest-track view goes from mean 22 to 12, and from 20 to 10.5, with\nthe wet track still glinting where the crowns open over it. On high\nthe dense forest hides most floor beyond the shadow map's reach (about\n62 m along the beam, 40 m across it), so it shows only on long\nsightlines. One vertex fetch and a few ALU per light; all presets\ncompile.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\ngit log --oneline -3","description":"Commit the canopy shading under flares"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
